"rpresser" <rpresser@gmail.com> wrote:

> The point is that if your steed is carrying the
> weight in the saddlebags, you get a bonus that is
> similar to the bonus from a bag of holding -- you
> can carry a lot more.

But that's already true merely from _riding_ a
steed, the PC becomes lots less burdened on
successfully mounting, and it's not clear that
saddlebags should make the total carrying capacity
of rider and steed any greater than if the rider has
all the loot in the (fairly virtual) "knapsack"
instead of split between knapsack and saddlebags.

The big win in carrying capacity is if the steed is
being led, as a pack animal, not ridden, in which
case the steed can probably carry lots more than the
(non-)rider can, from not having to carry the weight
of the rider, say a rider-weight plus the usual
rider-on-steed (some level of burdening) capacity,
and the (non-)rider can hirself carry a PC's
capacity in addition.

> Even if magic is not involved.

> Shouldn't that be balanced somehow with some
> danger?

Sure, to make you (and me) happy.

1) Since the RNG provides almost universally shoddy
goods, and PCs are always doing stuff in a hurry to
avoid being busy when attacked anyway, saddlebags
are probably both easily detatched from material
failure, and also from being poorly attached by the
PC, and should have some small per-turn chance of
falling off. To be nasty about it, that should be
"silent" -- not announced in the message window, and
left up to the player to notice on the screen.  [My
apologies in advance to blind NetHack players, to
whom this might be a horrid disadvantage.]

2) Accessing stuff from a saddlebag should probably
take more than one turn; think through the mechanics
of accessing that gear.

I still think this whole idea is pretty hugely
unbalancing, satisfying the same Monty Haul instinct
that led earlier to the addition of the sack (to
overcome knapsack menu limitations) and then the bag
of holding (to overcome PC strength limitations) to
increase the PC's carrying capacity out of all
reason and thus to remove much of the need for
excellent knapsack management skills in order to
ascend.

I.e., it's another way to "dumb down" NetHack. Grrr,

